    "narrative": "LawDog\u2019s lineup is anchored by **Daulton Varsho** (72.31) and **Tyler Freeman** (70.46), who represent our strongest plays of the day. **Daulton Varsho** draws a high-upside matchup against Quantrill, benefiting from a significant 19% SP skill boost and an ideal platoon advantage, while **Tyler Freeman** capitalizes on a favorable matchup against Paredes (5.42 xERA) and a prime #2 hole promotion. **Taylor Ward** also warrants attention; despite facing the tougher arm in Griffin, his 16% park factor boost and platoon edge make him a elite 67.92 start. Notably, our pursuit of stolen bases remains front-and-center, as **Masyn Winn**, **Matt Chapman**, **Jung Hoo Lee**, and **Drew Gilbert** all receive a 2% efficiency bump from facing pitchers with slow deliveries, helping to mitigate park or order-related regression for the bottom-third of our lineup.\n\nThe most difficult bench decision involved **Jeremiah Jackson**, whose 46.85 score keeps him on the outside looking in. While he benefits from the same hitter-friendly venue as **Taylor Ward**, his lower-tier batting order position (#8) and marginal platoon production compared to the utility floor of **Bryson Stott** (60.08) tipped the optimizer toward sitting him. We are currently navigating a significant amount of uncertainty with \"Lineup Pending\" designations across the board; should the projected batting orders shift downward for the likes of **Cal Raleigh** or **Jonathan Aranda**, those scores will fluctuate rapidly. We must also keep a close watch on the **Matt Chapman**, **Jung Hoo Lee**, and **Drew Gilbert** cluster; they are effectively relying on the projected wind-out conditions at their park to maintain their scoring threshold, and any weather-related pivot could render these marginal starts below our active roster floor. With **Jos\u00e9 Ram\u00edrez** remaining on the IL, we are relying on our depth to maintain the category leads in a thin SB environment.",
